      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (HP) is characterized by a granulomatous inflammation and may show various forms of clinical presentation, such as the acute, subacute, and chronic forms. The TH1-associated cytokines interleukin (IL) 12 and IL-18 and tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF-alpha) may be involved in the pathogenesis of both the acute and chronic forms of HP."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To compare the release of IL-12, IL-18, and TNF-alpha from bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) macrophages in these 2 forms of HP."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Patients underwent BAL 0 to 6 days after the last antigen exposure. Alveolar macrophages (AMs) from BAL in 6 patients with acute HP, 16 with chronic HP, and 11 controls were cultured for 24 hours. Cytokines in the culture supernatants were measured by enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The production of IL-12, IL-18, and TNF-alpha by AMs was increased in patients with both acute and chronic forms in either the absence or presence of lipopolysaccharide compared with controls. The levels of IL-12, IL-18, and TNF-alpha showed no difference between patients with acute and chronic HP. The spontaneous production of IL-12, IL-18, and TNF-alpha did not correlate with the CD4/CD8 ratio in BAL. The spontaneous and lipopolysaccharide-stimulated release of IL-12 showed a positive correlation with the percentage of lymphocytes (r = .470, P = .03; r = .496, P = .02; respectively) in BAL."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "This study demonstrates that an increased release of IL-12, IL-18, and TNF-alpha by AMs is associated with both the acute and chronic forms of HP."
        }
      ]},
